hi Luca,

That's an interesting question. We have our own data structures under the hood that are standard double precision. So there's no easy way to have NIMBLE itself do computations in higher precision.

If you ran in uncompiled mode, you could make use of Rmpfr functionality within your nimbleFunction run code. However, uncompiled execution will be quite slow, so we only recommend it for debugging. (Even on top of the overhead from running R code, use of nimble itself imposes some additional overhead...)

We have some relatively new functionality that allows one to call back to R or out to your own C/C++ code from within a nimbleFunction. So if you have a block of code where you need higher precision, you could do that via Rmpfr by calling back to R from within your nimbleFunction. The rest of the code in the nimbleFunction will still be compiled. Or you could call out to C/C++ code that uses higher precision (perhaps using whatever C code is under the hood in Rmpfr). That C/C++ code plus the rest of the nimbleFunction would all be compiled together. Please see "help(nimbleRcall)" or "help(nimbleExternalCall)".

Chris

