Yes, though it involves a bit of manual labor. Instead of using runMCMC or nimbleMCMC, you'll need to directly run the MCMC using our lower-level interface. Here's a basic skeleton:

## .... start with model building and mcmc setup steps ....
Cmcmc <- compileNimble(mcmc, project = model_object)

for(j in 1:nBlocks) {
   Cmcmc$run(niter = nIterPerBlock, reset = FALSE)
   samples <- as.matrix(Cmcmc$mvSamples)
   # insert code here to write to CSV
}

Note that doing this results in the object containing the samples (Cmcmc$mvSamples) growing as the blocks are added, so memory use will increase continually.

There's a slight chance that NIMBLE won't like the "reset = FALSE" for the first block, in which case you should just move the code for the first block of sampling outside of the for loop and remove the "reset = FALSE".

Chris

